{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>Summary</h2>

  <p class="lede">A download has three jobs: get the bytes, know how it is going, and put the
  bytes somewhere the user can find them. Every browser can do the first two. They differ
  almost entirely on the third.</p>

  <p>There are two ways to run a download in a browser. <strong>Hand it to the browser</strong>
  — give it a link and let its own download manager take over. This works everywhere, at any
  size, but the application is blind: no progress, no completion signal, and files land flat in
  the downloads folder. Or <strong>run it in the application</strong> — fetch the bytes and
  count them, which gives real progress and real completion, then put them somewhere. Where the
  application is permitted to put them is what varies by browser, and what decides the size it
  can handle.</p>

  <h3>The findings</h3>

  <table class="num">
    <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Finding</th><th>What follows from it</th></tr></thead>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td><strong>Staging in the browser's private storage costs the same at any file
          size.</strong> Measured at 22–92 MiB on Chromium and 15–25 MiB on Firefox, from
          64 MiB to 2 GiB.</td>
      <td>This is the mechanism to use. Its cost does not grow with the file, so it can move
          files larger than the machine's memory.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td><strong>Holding a file in memory costs the size of the file</strong> — 1.00× on
          Chromium, 1.62× on Firefox.</td>
      <td>Any approach that buffers the file caps the feature at the machine's memory, however
          much memory there is.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>3</td>
      <td><strong>Only Chromium can write into a folder the user chooses.</strong> Firefox and
          Safari both formally decline to implement it.</td>
      <td>Everywhere else, files arrive as a flat list. This will not change, so it is a fact
          to design around rather than wait out.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>4</td>
      <td><strong>Chrome for Android does have folder access</strong>, shipped in Chrome 132; a
          widely used compatibility database still reports otherwise and is stale.</td>
      <td>Android is more capable than the usual reference suggests, though defects reported at
          launch appear to remain open.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>5</td>
      <td><strong>Safari could not be measured</strong>, and iPhone has a page-memory ceiling
          around 100 MB with no catchable failure.</td>
      <td>Safari is treated as unverified rather than unsupported. Large downloads should not be
          attempted on iPhone at all.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  {{ c.note('The counterintuitive part',
     'Handing the file to the browser — the least capable option, and the one that reports
      nothing — is the only one with no size limit at all, because the application never holds
      the file. It therefore remains the right choice for the largest jobs, and the design keeps
      it rather than replacing it.') }}
</section>
